# ADR-017: Multi-Tenancy Strategy

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| ADR | 017 |
| Title | Multi-Tenancy Strategy |
| Status | Accepted |
| Date | 2026-07-27 |
| Decision Makers | R-zension Engineering Team |

---

# Context

R-zension is designed as a Software-as-a-Service (SaaS) platform where multiple organizations use the same application infrastructure.

Each organization (tenant) manages its own

- Users
- Goals
- Rankings
- Analytics
- Notifications
- AI Insights
- Reports

Data belonging to one organization must never be visible or accessible to another organization.

The platform therefore requires a secure, scalable, and maintainable multi-tenancy architecture.

---

# Decision

R-zension will implement a **Shared Database, Shared Schema** multi-tenancy model with **strict logical isolation**.

Every tenant-owned record contains an immutable `organizationId`.

All queries, APIs, services, caches, and AI requests must enforce tenant isolation.

---

# Architecture

```text
                    R-zension

                         │

        ┌────────────────┼────────────────┐

        ▼                ▼                ▼

 Organization A   Organization B   Organization C

        │                │                │

        └────────────────┼────────────────┘

                Shared Application

                       │

               Shared PostgreSQL

                       │

          Logical Isolation via organizationId
```

---

# Objectives

The multi-tenancy strategy should

- Ensure complete tenant isolation
- Support thousands of organizations
- Minimize operational cost
- Simplify deployments
- Enable horizontal scaling
- Support future enterprise deployments

---

# Tenant Definition

A tenant represents an independent organization using R-zension.

Each tenant owns

- Members
- Goals
- Rankings
- Reports
- Notifications
- AI Data
- Settings
- Analytics

---

# Tenant Identification

Every authenticated request contains

```text
organizationId
```

The backend derives the tenant context from the authenticated user.

Clients must never choose their own tenant context.

---

# Database Strategy

Every tenant-owned table includes

```text
organization_id
```

Examples

```text
users

goals

leaderboards

scores

notifications

analytics

reports
```

---

# Query Isolation

Every database query must include tenant filtering.

Correct

```sql
SELECT *
FROM goals
WHERE organization_id = :organizationId;
```

Incorrect

```sql
SELECT *
FROM goals;
```

No service may bypass tenant filtering.

---

# API Isolation

Every API endpoint must validate

- Authentication
- Organization membership
- Resource ownership
- Authorization

Access outside the authenticated tenant must return

```text
403 Forbidden
```

or

```text
404 Not Found
```

depending on the security policy.

---

# Cache Isolation

Redis keys must include tenant identifiers.

Example

```text
leaderboard:org:128

dashboard:org:128

analytics:org:128
```

This prevents cache leakage between organizations.

---

# AI Isolation

Oracle AI receives only tenant-scoped data.

Oracle must never access information from multiple organizations within a single request unless explicitly authorized for administrative analytics.

---

# File Isolation

Uploaded files belong to organizations.

Example storage structure

```text
organizations/

    org_128/

        reports/

        avatars/

        exports/
```

Authorization is validated before download.

---

# Notification Isolation

Notifications are tenant-specific.

Users receive notifications only from organizations they belong to.

---

# Audit Logging

Every audit entry includes

- organizationId
- userId
- action
- timestamp
- requestId

Audit records remain isolated per tenant.

---

# Background Jobs

Scheduled jobs execute within tenant boundaries.

Examples

- Weekly summaries
- Ranking recalculation
- Analytics generation
- Report exports

Jobs must never process multiple tenants in a single execution context unless explicitly designed for administrative purposes.

---

# Cross-Tenant Access

Cross-tenant access is prohibited.

Exceptions

- Platform Super Administrator
- Platform Monitoring
- System Maintenance
- Billing Services (Future)

Such access requires elevated privileges and complete audit logging.

---

# Organization Lifecycle

Supported operations

- Create
- Update
- Suspend
- Archive
- Restore
- Delete (Future)

Deletion should follow retention policies before permanent removal.

---

# Performance

Indexes should include

```text
organization_id
```

Common composite indexes

```text
organization_id, user_id

organization_id, rank

organization_id, created_at
```

This improves tenant-specific query performance.

---

# Monitoring

Track

- Active tenants
- Requests per tenant
- Storage usage
- AI usage
- Notification volume
- Cache usage
- Database performance

Per-tenant metrics enable operational insights.

---

# Security

Tenant isolation is enforced at every layer

- Authentication
- Authorization
- Database
- Cache
- File Storage
- AI
- Notifications
- Audit Logs

Security reviews should validate tenant isolation continuously.

---

# Backup Strategy

Backups include all tenant data.

Future enhancements may support

- Tenant-level restore
- Tenant migration
- Tenant export

---

# Scalability

The architecture should support

- Thousands of organizations
- Millions of users
- Horizontal application scaling
- Read replicas
- Distributed caching

Future migration to isolated databases should remain possible without major application redesign.

---

# Alternatives Considered

## Separate Database per Tenant

Pros

- Strong isolation
- Easy tenant backup
- Enterprise flexibility

Cons

- Higher infrastructure cost
- Operational complexity
- Difficult migrations
- Increased maintenance

Decision

Deferred for enterprise editions.

---

## Separate Schema per Tenant

Pros

- Better logical separation

Cons

- Schema management complexity
- Difficult upgrades
- Migration overhead

Decision

Rejected.

---

## Shared Database, Shared Schema

Pros

- Cost effective
- Simple deployment
- Easy scaling
- Mature SaaS architecture
- Efficient resource utilization

Decision

Accepted.

---

# Consequences

Positive

- Excellent scalability
- Lower operational costs
- Easier maintenance
- Simplified deployments
- Consistent architecture

Negative

- Strict discipline required for tenant filtering
- Additional testing requirements
- Strong authorization enforcement needed

---

# Future Evolution

Future enterprise capabilities include

- Dedicated databases
- Dedicated infrastructure
- Regional tenant placement
- Bring Your Own Database (BYOD)
- Tenant migration tooling
- Cross-region disaster recovery
- Enterprise compliance controls

The current architecture intentionally allows these capabilities to be introduced incrementally.

---

# Decision Summary

R-zension adopts a Shared Database, Shared Schema multi-tenancy model with strict logical isolation enforced through `organizationId`. Every application layer—including APIs, database queries, caching, AI, notifications, and file storage—must respect tenant boundaries, providing a scalable SaaS architecture while maintaining strong data isolation and security.

